hi there steve here steve kaufman today  i want to talk about output  speaking i know i always talk about the  importance of input and i believe very  much in the importance of input  but today  i want to talk about how do we get to  speak better because all of us who learn  languages are the majority of us  including myself although i talk a lot  about input my goal is to speak and to  speak well that's what i want to do  they can argue about the definitions of  fluency whatever but i want to speak  fluently i want to speak well  the so i'm going to talk i'm going to  mention maybe eight or nine points here  that i think are important first of all  in order to speak well you have to speak  a lot i think that's an important point  the languages that i speak the best are  the languages that i have spoken the  most so but it's not a matter of  speaking a lot now it's over the whole  length of time  that you spend or that you devote to  learning a language you have to have a  lot of opportunity to speak and i'll get  back to that so the first thing is to  speak well you have to speak a lot the  second point is i don't believe it  matters when you start speaking you can  start speaking early if you want to or  if you're in a situation where you need  to you're living in the country you have  to go to stores but if you don't it  doesn't matter because what really  matters is how much meaningful  conversation you have over the long haul  so what you do up front doesn't really  matter i put up a little greek  monologue the other day i don't think i  did very well i don't think i did much  better than i did two or three months  earlier it doesn't matter it doesn't  bother me i want to build up my  capability to eventually be fluent and  in the long run that means a lot of  meaningful conversation down the road  not necessarily what i can say after a  week or two or even a month  that's point number two  point number three is  the  interaction that you have to have with  the language  it's the same as as the  you know listening and reading that we  do it has to be meaningful it has to be  authentic and therefore i don't think  the amount of time we spend speaking in  a classroom  really counts that much  role playing in the classroom  or even when we're speaking to other  non-native speakers that's not the same  as being immersed in an environment with  native speakers if you have a lot of  opportunity to do that your spoken  language is going to improve a lot  okay so the fourth point i'd like to  make is if you don't have that  opportunity don't be too hard on  yourself a lot of people you know i say  i wish i could speak better and they're  very critical  very critical of their own capabilities  but they haven't if they haven't been in  a situation  where they can where they're with native  speakers or they're listening to a lot  of stuff and they're interacting with  people and in a natural environment if  they haven't been in that situation you  know sustained conversations evening  after evening things like that then of  course their language is going to be  somewhat limited not going to meet their  expectations so don't be tough on  yourself give yourself credit for what  you can do in fact  i find that people are overly critical  of their own ability in in a language  ability to speak but they sometimes tend  to  uh  overestimate their ability  to re to read and listen their  comprehension skills because i've been  in many situations where people say to  me you know i can read and listen my  comprehension is very good but i just  can't speak  reality is in many cases that they speak  not so badly  but if i ask them do you read novels  usually the answer is no they would find  it difficult to read a novel from page  from cover to cover  do you understand movies  um  you know do you understand audiobooks  and usually the answer is no so  that means that they've still got a long  way to go in their comprehension  ability  and if they did have really strong  comprehension ability in other words  they did understand movies and audio  books and they read novels then their  speaking would develop very quickly so  what that says to me is that  if you don't if you're not immersed in  the language if you're not in that  environment what you have to do is to  continue to build up your comprehension  skills through intensive listening and  reading activity  and  you know there's sort of two kinds of  listening and reading you can be  extensive or intensive extensive means  that you're covering a lot of new  material you're stimulating your mind  you're engaging with interesting content  you're discovering new vocabulary  sometimes it's hard  going and you need to do that in order  to increase your vocabulary increase  your familiarity with the language but  sometimes it's a good idea maybe 20 of  the time to do more intensive listening  to listen to the same material over and  over again listen to it read it save  words and phrases listen to it again  listen to it 10 15 20 times i find that  this builds up my capability in terms of  speaking  because when i speak i use a small  percentage of the sort of passive  vocabulary that i have so i it's a it's  a way of building up your capability to  use  you know those words that you're going  to activate so i would vary again  to prepare yourself i would vary  you know extensive listening and reading  with intensive  listening and reading and this is a way  to prepare yourself for that immersion  experience because don't forget just  going to that immersion experience is no  guarantee that you'll be able to take  advantage of it i see all the time i see  immigrants who come to canada and if  they don't have a fairly good level of  english english before they arrive they  never ever learn to speak properly so  when you are  given that immersion opportunity you  want to be prepared so obviously a lot  of listening and reading helps to  prepare you but there are other things  also that we can do  obviously the classroom speaking in the  classroom is however artificial it may  be it's one way of  improving you know your preparedness so  that you'll be able to cope with a  with a real you know  genuine interaction situation uh  conversations on skype i do that uh i  don't do a lot of it i might i don't do  it in the initial period but eventually  i might get to doing again you know  doing one or two a week and after each  discussion i get a list of the phrases  and words that i didn't use properly and  i studied those some tutors actually  record these for me which i think is  also  very good  so that's something that you can do to  uh  to prepare yourself for the immersion  experience speaking with uh other  non-native speakers and you know uh  polyglot clubs is also a way to prepare  yourself but it's not the same as that  full immersion experience with natives  other things you can do um  you can for example record yourself on  youtube as i did you can leave them  private but that can track your growth  in the language plus when you listen to  yourself it's an opportunity for you to  see where your gaps are and realize  where you need to improve because  there's no way the teacher is going to  you know correct all of your mistakes  so those are some things that you can do  to prepare yourself  writing is another way to prepare  yourself obviously for speaking because  you have more time when you're writing  you can look up  grammar rules to help you out  you can use google translate  and then continue writing so these are  all things that you can do  along with your input activities to  prepare yourself so that ultimately when  you are given that opportunity to be  immersed in the language you can take  full advantage and ultimately in order  to speak really well you do have to have  a lot of opportunity to speak in the  language and you want to be able to take  full advantage of it so there you have  it my thoughts on speaking bye for now  [Music]  you
 

